$ExtraRowsEn = @(
    '| General | Path to `AtlasServer.exe` | – | Plugin (registry `ServerPath`) | yes | Choose file, show version | #12 |',
    '| Check rules | Unit cycles while typing (`diagnostics.usesCycleLive`): rule `uses-cycle` in the editor with the shortest path at the triggering uses entry, an error when every edge lies in the interface (F2047); every unit in the hover jumps to its uses entry, “Show graph” opens the unit relations; quick fix “Move to implementation uses” (sorted with `sortUses`) when no identifier of the unit is needed in the interface section, otherwise jumps to those identifiers; also cycles with an implementation edge as a hint (`diagnostics.usesCycleImplementation`); off = no message in the editor, the project-wide check (#64) stays | on, off | atlas.json (Atlas › Project settings, page Check rules) | no | Checkbox, checkbox | #896 |',
    '| Compiler | Builds for Claude Code (`compiler.mcpBuild`, quick toggle): the MCP tool `build_project` may build open projects in this IDE through IOTAProjectBuilder (make, build, check; configuration and platform only for this build) and receives the result and compiler messages, also in the Starter Edition without a command-line compiler. Refused with a reason while the debugger, another build or the compiler check is running; off = the IDE accepts no requests | on | Plugin (registry `McpBuild`) | no | Checkbox | #261 |',
    '| Compiler | Run DUnitX tests (#69, `tests.run`, quick toggle): commands “Run tests” (*Atlas.RunTests*) and “Re-run failed tests” (*Atlas.RunFailedTests*) in the command palette; the test project is the active one or one of the project group whose main file uses `DUnitX.TestFramework` (for a single test the test project that contains its test unit); with several candidates Atlas asks once and remembers the choice for exactly these candidates (#585, locally in `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Atlas\test-projects.json`); platform and configuration from the test project; result via the XML logger of DUnitX, failed tests as problems at the test method (source `DUnitX`, expected and actual value) and in the messages view; off = commands, code lens and the run after the build disappear | on | Plugin (registry `TestRun`) | no | Checkbox | #69 |',
    '| Compiler | Build before the tests (`tests.buildFirst`): “Run tests” builds the test project first (make) like `build_project`; off = the existing test EXE runs without the compiler | on | Plugin (registry `TestsBuildFirst`) | no | Checkbox | #69 |',
    '| Compiler | Tests after the build (`tests.afterBuild`, quick toggle): after every successful build of a DUnitX test project started by the user its tests run (compile events #527); not after the background check and not after builds for Claude Code | off | Plugin (registry `TestsAfterBuild`) | no | Checkbox | #69 |',
    '| Compiler | Run affected tests (#1353, `tests.affected` in settings.json, quick toggle; depth `tests.affectedDepth`): command “Run affected tests” (*Atlas.RunAffectedTests*, Atlas menu and command palette) asks the server for the tests reached by the changes against the base (Git: merge base with main, SVN: BASE; including uncommitted changes) and runs only those like “Run tests”; if all are needed (project file changed, analysis incomplete), all run. Offer before the commit (`tests.affectedBeforeCommit`): Atlas installs a pre-commit hook in `.git/hooks` that names the affected tests and runs them in the terminal on request (Git only; Atlas leaves a foreign hook alone) | on, 3, off | settings.json (`tests.affected`, `tests.affectedDepth`), Plugin (registry `TestsAffectedBeforeCommit`) | no | Checkbox, number, checkbox | #1353 |',
    '| Compiler | Build only successful without warnings and hints (`build.zeroHints`): a build with counted warnings or hints counts as failed (red summary, red entry in the notification center, no tests after the build); messages in the baseline do not count | off | Plugin (registry `BuildZeroHints`) | no | Checkbox | #530 |',
    '| Compiler | Cost of an interface change in the status bar (#554, `build.showInterfaceCost`): when the caret is in the interface section of a unit, the extra panel shows “interface change: N units will be recompiled” (hint: the first units; click: “Dependencies of this unit”); in the implementation section, with 0 units and without the server no panel; needs `architecture.enabled` (the same uses graph); off = no panel, the commands “Dependencies of this unit” and “Most expensive units for interface changes” stay | on | Plugin (registry `ShowInterfaceCost`) | no | Checkbox | #554 |',
    '| Compiler | Summary after every build (`build.summary`) in the Atlas group of the messages window, e.g. “Build Kasse succeeded, but 2 warnings, 1 hint”, with the first message to jump to; a build that `build.zeroHints` or `build.warningsAsErrors` make fail is reported by Atlas even without this setting | on | Plugin (registry `BuildSummary`) | no | Checkbox | #530 |',
    '| Compiler | Codes that count as errors (`build.warningsAsErrors`): warnings and hints such as `W1035, H2164`; if a build contains one of them (outside the baseline), it counts as failed | empty | Plugin (registry `BuildWarningsAsErrors`) | no | Text field | #530 |',
    '| Compiler | Check before the compile (`build.gate`: off, warn, block): before every make and build started by the user (not Code Insight, background check, syntax check or requests from Claude Code) Atlas checks the units changed since the last successful compile with the IDE buffers (also unsaved) for syntax errors and rule findings with severity error; the findings appear in the Atlas group of the messages window (double-click jumps there). off = no check, warn = report, block = cancel the compile; a second compile without changes within 10 s runs anyway (deliberate override). The same applies to the release build guard (#533), where off and warn only report | off | Plugin (registry `BuildGate`) | no | Choice | #529, #533 |',
    '| Compiler | Time limit of the check before the compile (`build.gateTimeoutMs`, 50 to 10000 ms): if the check takes longer, the compile runs unchecked and Atlas reports this once per session | 300 | Plugin (registry `BuildGateTimeoutMs`) | no | Number | #529 |',
    '| Compiler | Window “Compiler switches” (`build.compilerSwitches`, quick toggle): dockable window with the effective compiler switches at the cursor position: `R`, `Q`, `O`, `C`, `I`, `A`, `HINTS`, `WARNINGS`, warnings switched off or reported as errors (`{$WARN … OFF}`), active symbols as well as platform and configuration. For each value its origin: project option (property of the .dproj), compiler default, include file or directive with line (`R−, set in line 10`). Deviations from the .dproj are highlighted. Computed by the server through the preprocessor up to the cursor line (`atlas/compilerSwitches`), 150 ms after the last cursor movement. Double-click or Enter jumps to the directive or opens the project options. Command `Atlas.CompilerSwitches` | on | Plugin (registry `BuildCompilerSwitches`) | no | Checkbox | #558 |',
    '| Compiler | Deviating compiler switches in the status bar (`build.showSwitchesInStatusBar`): extra panel in the editor status bar with the switches that deviate from the .dproj at the cursor position, such as `R− Q− HINTS OFF` or `WARN SYMBOL_PLATFORM OFF`; without a deviation no panel appears. The tooltip names the origin, a click opens the window “Compiler switches”. Only works with `build.compilerSwitches` | off | Plugin (registry `BuildSwitchesInStatusBar`) | no | Checkbox | #558 |',
    '| Compiler | Start profiles (#575, `startProfiles.enabled`, quick toggle): command “Start profile: …” (*Atlas.StartProfile*, command palette) and a click on the status bar panel choose for the active project a profile from `startProfiles` of the project file `.atlas` (shared, needs `teamFile.enabled`) or a personal one; Atlas sets parameters, working folder, host application and environment variables of the active configuration and platform (like *Run › Parameters*), *Run* and *Run without debugging* then use them; the active profile applies per project and is set again when the project, configuration or platform changes; “No start profile” restores the run options from before the first profile; “Save current run options as personal profile …”; panel “Start profile: <name>” in the status bar as soon as the project has profiles (`startProfiles.statusField`, quick toggle); off = command, panel and tip disappear, the run options stay as they are | on, on | Plugin (registry `StartProfiles`, `StartProfilesStatusField`); personal profiles, active profile and the previous run options per project only locally in `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Atlas\startprofiles\<key>.json`; shared ones in `.atlas` | no | Checkbox, checkbox | #575 |',
    '| Compiler | Build once on opening (`build.onOpen`, quick toggle; kind `build.onOpenKind`: `check` syntax check, `make` compile, `build` build): when a project or project group is opened (also the one loaded when the IDE starts), Atlas builds it once with the IDE compiler as soon as the IDE is idle (no input for `build.onOpenDelayMs`, no build, no debugger, no compiler check); with the IDE background compilation switched on without blocking, otherwise with its progress dialog. Once per opening; tests after the build do not run, the release guard checks as with every build. Result per project in the notification center, with several projects additionally a summary (source `openBuild`); off = no build on opening | off, make | Plugin (registry `BuildOnOpen`, `BuildOnOpenKind`) | no | Choice “do not build / syntax check / compile / build” |  |',
    '| Compiler | Scope in a project group (`build.onOpenScope`: `all` all projects in the order of the group, `active` only the active project) | all | Plugin (registry `BuildOnOpenScope`) | no | Choice |  |',
    '| Compiler | Delay on opening (`build.onOpenDelayMs`, 500 to 60000 ms): this long after the last opening and after the last input before Atlas builds | 3000 | Plugin (registry `BuildOnOpenDelayMs`) | no | Number |  |',
    '| Compiler | Do not build on opening (`build.onOpenExclude`, project names without extension, separated by commas), e.g. a package installed in this IDE | – | Plugin (registry `BuildOnOpenExclude`) | no | Text field |  |',
    '| Compiler | Configuration on opening (`build.onOpenConfig`, e.g. Debug; empty = as in the project): Atlas builds every project with this configuration, whatever is set in the project, and restores the selection afterwards; if a project does not know it, the project''s setting applies there (note in the summary) | – | Plugin (registry `BuildOnOpenConfig`) | no | Text field | #1293 |',
    '| Compiler | Target platform on opening (`build.onOpenPlatform`, e.g. Win64; empty = as in the project): like the configuration; if the platform is not enabled in a project, the project''s setting applies there | – | Plugin (registry `BuildOnOpenPlatform`) | no | Text field | #1293 |',
    '| Release build | Release build guard (`build.releaseGuard`, quick toggle): before make and build in a release configuration Atlas checks the project options as the running IDE uses them for the build (with inherited values, also unsaved; not the .dproj on disk), according to `build.releaseChecks`. Violations appear in the Atlas group of the messages window with rule ID and a hint where to change the option. Also applies to builds through the runner (Claude Code `build_project`, “Open and build” #764), never to debug configurations, the background check, syntax check, clean or Code Insight. Command “Check release build” (*Atlas.CheckRelease*, command palette) checks the active project without a build. Result and version of every successful release build are stored in the section `release` of the project summary; off = no check and no command | on | Plugin (registry `ReleaseGuard`) | no | Checkbox | #533 |',
    '| Release build | Release configurations (`build.releaseConfigs`, separated by commas; “Release” always applies, case does not matter) | Release | Plugin (registry `ReleaseConfigs`) | no | Text field | #533 |',
    '| Release build | Checks of the guard (`build.releaseChecks`, separated by commas; `-name` switches one off): `debugDefine` (symbol DEBUG), `debugInfo` (debug information; with target value off also “Link with debug information”), `stackFrames`, `assertions`, `rangeChecks`, `overflowChecks` (each with target value `=off` or `=on`; if the option is missing, the compiler default applies), `memoryLeaks` (`ReportMemoryLeaksOnShutdown := True` in the project source outside `{$IFDEF DEBUG}`), `versionInfo` (no version information or the same version as in the last successful release build of the same configuration and platform; not with an auto-incremented build number), `outputFolder` (output folder equal to that of the Debug configuration). Packages without `memoryLeaks` and `outputFolder`. Rule IDs `release-debug-define` to `release-output-folder` | all on, target values off | Plugin (registry `ReleaseChecks`) | no | Checkbox or choice per check | #533 |',
    '| Debugging | Debug a single test (#585, `tests.debug`, quick toggle): commands “Debug test at cursor” (*Atlas.DebugTest*) and “Run test at cursor” (*Atlas.RunTest*, with `tests.run`) in the command palette, entries “Debug test”/“Run test” in the editor context menu and the mark “Debug” of the code lens at `[TestFixture]`, `[Test]` and each `[TestCase]`; Atlas builds the test project (like “Run tests”), temporarily sets the run parameters `--run:<Unit.Fixture.Method[.Case]>` through the start profiles (#575) (existing parameters stay, `--run`, `--include`, `--exclude`, `--xmlfile` and `--exitbehavior` give way) and starts it in the debugger; afterwards the active project and run parameters are as before (also after a crash at the next start), the result appears as with “Run tests”. Needs “Run tests” and `codeLens.tests`; off = command, entry, mark and tip disappear | on | Plugin (registry `TestDebug`) | no | Checkbox | #585 |',
    '| Debugging | Stop at the start of the method when debugging a test (`debug.testBreakAtStart`): one-time breakpoint on the first statement of the test method, which Atlas removes after the first stop (an own breakpoint there stays untouched); not when debugging a whole fixture | on | Plugin (registry `TestBreakAtStart`) | no | Checkbox | #585 |',
    '| Debugging | Variable values in the hover (`debug.hoverValues`, quick toggle): while the process is paused, Atlas evaluates the expression under the mouse (identifier with the qualifiers to its left, e.g. `FList.Count`; nothing in strings, comments and with calls) through the IDE debugger without side effects and shows the value at the top of the Atlas tooltip; if the debugger answers with a delay (Win64), it shows “evaluating …”; with the own tooltip off Atlas passes the value to the IDE tooltip | on | Plugin (registry `DebugHoverValues`) | no | Checkbox | #891 |',
    '| Debugging | Explain “no access” while debugging (`debug.explainUnavailable`, quick toggle): if evaluating a value in the hover or in the values in the editor fails with a known IDE text (German and English, such as “Inaccessible value” or “… not … because of optimization”; own additions in `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Atlas\debug-unavailable.txt`), it shows `‹no access›`. The hover names the cause in a fixed order: started in a configuration without debug information, `$D-`/`$L-` at the location, `$O+` (variable in a register), lifetime exceeded or not yet begun (approximation), routine `inline`, library frame without debug DCUs; plus the origin of the switch and fixes with preview: Restart with Debug, `{$OPTIMIZATION OFF}`/`ON` around the routine, `{$O-}` at the start of the unit, use debug DCUs, go to the directive. Without a match “Cause unknown” with the checked items | on | Plugin (registry `DebugExplainUnavailable`) | no | Checkbox | #840 |',
    '| Debugging | Completion in debugger dialogs (`debug.completionInDialogs`, quick toggle): in “Evaluate/Modify” (Ctrl+F7), in the expression of a watch and in the condition and “Eval expression” of a breakpoint Atlas opens its own popup with identifiers from the scope: local variables, parameters, fields of `Self`, units and global names, after `.` the members. The scope is the execution point while the program is paused, its line in the breakpoint dialog, otherwise the cursor line in the editor. The list opens at the first letter of a word and after `.`, Ctrl+Space opens it by hand. Arrow down in the empty field shows the last 20 expressions of the project (`%LOCALAPPDATA%\Atlas\debug-expressions.json`) | on | Plugin (registry `DebugCompletionInDialogs`) | no | Checkbox | #538 |',
    '| Debugging | Window “Evaluate” (`debug.evaluateWindow`, quick toggle): dockable window “Atlas: Evaluate” (command `Atlas.DebugEvaluate`): Enter adds an expression, Del removes it; at every stop Atlas re-evaluates all rows in time slices and marks values that changed since the last stop. Objects, records and arrays expand, the fields come from the Atlas index (without the server from the result string) and are evaluated one by one. Below, the selected value as text (control characters visible), lines (`Expression.Text`), JSON or hex; “Modify …” (F2) sets a new value. Ctrl+Enter pins an expression (`%LOCALAPPDATA%\Atlas\debug-evaluate-pinned.json`, per project); arrow down in the empty field shows the last 50 expressions (`debug-evaluate-history.json`). “Allow side effects” (off) permits function calls and getters | on | Plugin (registry `DebugEvaluateWindow`) | no | Checkbox | #784 |',
    '| Debugging | Ctrl+F7 opens the Atlas window (`debug.evaluateWindow.replaceCtrlF7`): Atlas hooks into the IDE action “Evaluate/Modify”: Ctrl+F7 opens the window “Evaluate” with the single-line selection or the expression under the cursor (`Kunde.Adresse.Ort`) in the input field. With Shift held down (also when clicking in the “Run” menu) and with the command `Atlas.DebugEvaluateIdeDialog` the IDE opens its dialog. Off = Ctrl+F7 stays unchanged | on | Plugin (registry `DebugEvaluateReplaceCtrlF7`) | no | Checkbox | #784 |',
    '| Debugging | Explain access violations (`debug.explainAccessViolation`, quick toggle): when stopping because of an access violation in own code, Atlas evaluates the access chains of the line from the left and names in the exception bar (otherwise in the messages) the nil member with field and offset, freed objects (fill pattern) and indexes outside a dynamic array; the nil member is underlined in the editor | on | Plugin (registry `DebugExplainAccessViolation`) | no | Checkbox | #865 |',
    '| Debugging | Allow getters and function calls (`debug.explainAccessViolation.allowCalls`): members with a getter, function call or default property are evaluated by Atlas only with this setting (the debugger then calls code in the process); off, they are listed as “not evaluated” | off | Plugin (registry `DebugExplainAccessViolationAllowCalls`) | no | Checkbox | #865 |',
    '| Debugging | Step filters when stepping into (`debug.stepFilters`, quick toggle): F7 skips own routines of the chosen kinds (getters and setters with one statement, trivial routines, class operators, constructors and destructors that only call inherited) and methods by pattern: Atlas runs the routine until it returns and then steps into the next call of the same statement or onto the next line. Shift+F7 (and “Step into anyway” in the command palette) ignores the filters for one step; breakpoints in skipped routines still trigger. Context menu “Skip when stepping into” / “Stop skipping”, window “Step filters” with matches from the index | on | Plugin (registry `DebugStepFilters`) | no | Checkbox | #850 |',
    '| Debugging | Exception history while debugging (`debug.exceptionHistory`, quick toggle): every stop because of an exception goes into the dockable window “Atlas: Exceptions” (command `Atlas.DebugExceptions`) with time, class, message (from the IDE exception dialog), count, location (first frame with source) and thread; equal exceptions at the same location are counted; filter by class and text, Enter jumps to the location, Ctrl+C copies class, message and call stack; “Ignore class from now on” (list `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Atlas\debug-ignore.json`) silently continues past the dialog for this class; at most 1000 entries per session | on | Plugin (registry `DebugExceptionHistory`) | no | Checkbox | #517 |',
    '| Debugging | Just my code while debugging (`debug.justMyCode`, quick toggle): own code are the units below the project folders of the project group and below `debug.ownCodeFolders`. After an exception in library code (RTL, VCL, components) Atlas jumps, once the exception dialog is closed, to the first own line in the call stack and marks it; if F7 (Trace into) lands in code without source, Atlas continues with “Run until return” until source is reached again (at most 16 times); in the window “Atlas: Exceptions” the location is the first own frame, the call stack shows library frames dimmed and folds two or more into “… n library frames”; command `Atlas.DebugJustMyCode` toggles it | on | Plugin (registry `DebugJustMyCode`) | no | Checkbox | #578 |',
    '| Debugging | Exception bar instead of the dialog (`debug.exceptionBanner`, quick toggle): builds on `debug.exceptionHistory`. When the debugger stops because of an exception, a bar appears at the top of the editor: class, message, thread, how often the class occurred at this location, call stack shortened to the first own line (“n levels hidden”, the line is marked), buttons Break (F8/Esc), Continue (F9), Ignore class, Ignore only here, Copy message. In Delphi 12 and 13 Atlas closes the IDE exception dialog via “Break”; in other versions or with the command `Atlas.DebugExceptionIdeDialog` the dialog stays and the bar appears after it (note in the status panel). Default off until the study (docs/Studie-Exception-Dialog.md) is positive | off | Plugin (registry `DebugExceptionBanner`) | no | Checkbox | #783 |',
    '| Debugging | Ignore only here (`debug.exceptionBanner.ignoreAtLocation`): button of the exception bar: remembers class + unit + line per project in `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Atlas\debug-ignore.json`; if the same combination comes again, Atlas continues silently and only counts in the exception history. At another line the debugger still stops. Off = no button, remembered locations have no effect | on | Plugin (registry `DebugExceptionBannerIgnoreAtLocation`) | no | Checkbox | #783 |',
    '| Debugging | Values in the editor while debugging (`debug.inlineValues`, quick toggle): while the process is paused (breakpoint, single step, pause), the server names the local variables, parameters, loop variables and fields of `Self` of the routine with the execution point, from the start of the routine to the current line (`textDocument/inlineValue`); Atlas evaluates them without side effects through the IDE debugger (never calls, properties or fields in `with`) and writes them dimmed after the end of the line: `Count = 3, Name = ''Meier''`. Values changed since the last stop are highlighted, long values shortened, `TDateTime`/`TDate`/`TTime` as date or time. Evaluation runs in time slices of at most 30 ms so that F8 does not become noticeably slower; on continuing the values disappear | on | Plugin (registry `DebugInlineValues`) | no | Checkbox | #508 |',
